% ============================================================================
% fable-moral — each fable's own explicitly stated moral (adj-facts-stdlib,
% LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% The NINTH literacy sub-skill library in this loop's sweep, and the
% FIRST to ground a whole-TEXT comprehension artifact rather than a
% word-level phonics/spelling fact: a classic fable's own stated moral,
% the lesson the narrator draws at the story's end. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? fable_moral(tortoise_and_the_hare, $Moral)
%       % "Slow but steady wins the race."
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `fable_moral(fable, moral)` carrying the source citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the moral WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on any fable the source does not give a standalone moral for.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind both variables to enumerate every
% fable/moral pair the table grounds:
%
%     ? fable_moral($F, $Moral)
%       % tortoise_and_the_hare/"Slow but steady...", shepherds_boy_and_the_wolf/"There is no believing...", boy_and_the_filberts/"Do not attempt..."
%
% (NOTE: the ADJ query grammar accepts a quoted-string LITERAL as a row
% VALUE inside a `table` block, but not as a QUERY argument — a query can
% only ground an atom/number or bind a $Var, so recalling "which fable
% has moral X" is done by enumerating with $F, $Moral and reading off the
% match, not by querying with the moral string as a ground argument.)
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why only THREE fables, out of many candidates checked: a
% classic Aesop translation does not uniformly close every fable with a
% standalone "moral" sentence in the narrator's own voice — some fables
% instead end with a character's spoken line that CARRIES a lesson but is
% still that character's dialogue, not the narrator's stated moral. This
% library only tables the THREE fables where the cited translation gives
% a clean, unambiguous, narrator-voice closing moral, verified by reading
% the raw page text directly:
%
%     fable                        moral (verbatim, narrator's voice)
%     ---------------------------  -----------------------------------------------------------------
%     tortoise_and_the_hare        "Slow but steady wins the race."
%     shepherds_boy_and_the_wolf   "There is no believing a liar, even when he speaks the truth."
%     boy_and_the_filberts         "Do not attempt too much at once."
%
% Three OTHER fables on the same page were deliberately excluded after
% verification because their closing line is a CHARACTER's spoken
% dialogue, not the narrator's own stated moral: "The Ants and the
% Grasshopper" (the ants' taunt to the grasshopper), "The Fox and the
% Crow" (the fox's gloating remark to the crow), and "The Lion and the
% Mouse" (the mouse's own words to the lion). Asserting those as this
% fable's "moral" the same way the three above are stated would overclaim
% what the source actually does — a character's dialogue is not
% automatically the narrator's stated lesson.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from George Fyler Townsend's classic English
% translation of Aesop's Fables, hosted by Project Gutenberg — a
% legitimate public-domain literary primary source. WebFetch-verified
% (including a targeted re-fetch distinguishing narrator's-voice morals
% from character dialogue) before writing. `trust authoritative` — a
% primary literary text, the same tier this stdlib gives other primary
% public-domain/government sources. A fable not one of these three — e.g.
% `the_fox_and_the_crow`, a real fable on the same page but excluded for
% the reason above — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on
% it rather than inventing or misattributing a moral. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table fable_moral {
    columns fable, moral

    row (tortoise_and_the_hare, "Slow but steady wins the race.")
    row (shepherds_boy_and_the_wolf, "There is no believing a liar, even when he speaks the truth.")
    row (boy_and_the_filberts, "Do not attempt too much at once.")

    source "Slow but steady wins the race."
    locator "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/21/old/aesop11h.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
